Koru has numbers now — not machine floats with better marketing, but a surface where "number" means something a proof can hold onto. Four modules carry it: `std/num` (arbitrary-precision decimals, exact or externally tainted), `std/quantity` (SI dimensions and uncertainty with covariance), `std/sym` (canonical symbolic expressions), and the certificate lane on top of it — whose semantics are machine-checked in Lean 4 ([`HolonomicClosure.lean`](https://github.com/Cypoe/ISAR-proofs/blob/main/src/ISAR/HolonomicClosure.lean), [`HolonomicBridge.lean`](https://github.com/Cypoe/ISAR-proofs/blob/main/src/ISAR/HolonomicBridge.lean), [`QuantityKernel.lean`](https://github.com/Cypoe/ISAR-proofs/blob/main/src/ISAR/QuantityKernel.lean), and friends). The newest surface, `sym.rel`, is where relationships between abstract generators live.

## std/num — exact means exact

A `std/num` value is an arbitrary-precision decimal — mantissa as a big integer plus a base-10 exponent — and it knows whether it is *exact* or *external*. A literal is exact; text ingested from the outside world is tainted until `ingest` discharges it, and arithmetic on tainted values refuses rather than laundering provenance:

```koru
import std/io
import std/num

std/num:lit(s: "123456789012345678901234567890")
| exact a |> std/num:lit(s: "987654321098765432109876543210")
    | exact b |> std/num:add(a, b): r |> std/io:print.ln("{{ r:s }}")
    | bad x |> std/io:print.ln("bad")
| bad y |> std/io:print.ln("bad")
```

<RegressionTestLink
