    { name: "615_008 ingest taint", directory: "615_008_num_ingest_taint", categorySlug: "600_STDLIB/615_NUM", description: "External text enters tainted; the obligation gates arithmetic." },
    { name: "615_011 untainted operand refusal", directory: "615_011_reject_untainted_operand", categorySlug: "600_STDLIB/615_NUM", description: "Arithmetic on an undischargeable operand refuses by name." }
  ]}
/>

The operation surface is the one a calculator wants — `add`/`sub`/`mul`/`div`, `pow`, `sqrt`, `exp`, `ln`, `sin`/`cos`/`atan`, `powf`, `mod`, base conversion — with fixed decimal precision requested per call, never machine precision smuggled in. The multiplication underneath is Koru's own limb-level Karatsuba (Karatsuba & Ofman, 1962): the race against `bc` and Python's `decimal` (`tests/benchmarks/016_num_precision_race`, replayable) showed Zig's stock schoolbook `mul` was the bottleneck, so the limb layer got owned — transcendentals dropped roughly an order of magnitude, and ReleaseFast beats `bc` on all ten workloads, including a Machin-formula π (1706, still the fast classroom recipe — the `fxPi` constant itself runs Chudnovsky binary-splitting). The C bigint implementations still win the pure exact-op races; the honest remaining gap is there in the results table, not smoothed over.

## std/quantity — dimensions are a group, not a string

`std/quantity` packs a magnitude, a canonical `Dim`, and optional uncertainty-with-covariance into one linear string. `Dim` is `[7]i32` — the free abelian group on the seven SI base generators, the same object [`QuantityKernel.lean`](https://github.com/Cypoe/ISAR-proofs/blob/main/src/ISAR/QuantityKernel.lean) proves things about — so `m/s · s → m` is group law, not string surgery — and juxtaposition is multiplication, so `lit(5 m/s)`, `lit(5m/s)` and `lit(5*m/s)` spell the same quantity:

```koru
import std/io
import std/quantity

std/quantity:lit(5 m/s)
| exact v |> std/quantity:lit(3 s)
    | exact t |> std/quantity:mul(a: v, b: t)
        | exact d |> std/quantity:render(n: d): o |> std/io:print.ln("{{ o:s }}")
        | mixed-cov |> std/io:print.ln("mixed-cov")
        | bad x |> std/io:print.ln("{{ x:s }}")
        | sym-unc |> std/io:print.ln("sym-unc")
    | bad x |> std/io:print.ln("bad")
| bad y |> std/io:print.ln("bad")
```

prints `1.5e1|m^1 s^0 kg^0 A^0 K^0 mol^0 cd^0|-` — fifteen metres, dimensionally normal by construction. `m/s ÷ s` lands `m^1 s^-2`, and `4 N · 2 m` renders the same canonical dim string as `8 J`, so `equiv` on quantities is string equality. A unit mismatch on `add` refuses `dim-mismatch`; quantity text that never passed `lit` can't reach the ops at all — the same `external`/`exact` mark discharge `std/num` runs, applied at the pack boundary.

### Case study: uncertainty is arithmetic, not annotation

A quantity's third field is a linearized (GUM-adjacent) sigma. Two independent measurements combine in quadrature — `lit(2.0 kg, unc: 0.1) + lit(1.0 kg, unc: 0.1)` renders `3e0|…kg…|1.41e-1`, the √2·0.1 that quadrature demands. Under `mul`/`div` the relative sigmas combine, and the magnitude stays a `std/num` decimal underneath, so the precision budget is the caller's, not the machine's.

