/>

### Case study: shared-source covariance cancels honestly

An uncertainty can be tagged with the source it stems from: `unc: 0.1, cov: bias`. Two `@bias` sigmas are fully correlated, so they add *linearly* — `2 m ± 0.1@bias + 1 m ± 0.1@bias` renders `3e0|…|2e-1@bias`, not the quadrature 0.14. The interesting arm is subtraction: `2 m ± 0.1@bias − 1 m ± 0.1@bias` renders `1e0|…|-` — the shared bias cancels to nothing and the difference is *certain*, which is the correct physics of a common-mode error. And two different sources refuse: `@bias + @other` answers `mixed-cov`, because the correlation between two unknowably-related sources cannot be assumed — the lane names its boundary rather than laundering independence into the result.

```koru
std/quantity:lit(2 m, unc: 0.1, cov: bias)
| exact a |> std/quantity:lit(1 m, unc: 0.1, cov: bias)
    | exact b |> std/quantity:add(a, b)
        | exact r |> std/quantity:render(n: r): t |> std/io:print.ln("{{ t:s }}")
        | dim-mismatch |> std/io:print.ln("dim-mismatch")
        | mixed-cov |> std/io:print.ln("mixed-cov")
    | bad x |> std/io:print.ln("bad")
| bad y |> std/io:print.ln("bad")
```

prints `3e0|m^1 s^0 kg^0 A^0 K^0 mol^0 cd^0|2e-1@bias`. Run the same pair through `sub` instead and the render is `1e0|m^1 s^0 kg^0 A^0 K^0 mol^0 cd^0|-` — the sigma field is literally empty, because the shared bias cancelled. Swap one `cov: bias` for `cov: other` and the same `add` refuses on the `mixed-cov` arm.

<RegressionTestLink
  tests={[
    { name: "615_030 covariance arms", directory: "615_030_quantity_covariance", categorySlug: "600_STDLIB/615_NUM", description: "Same-group adds linearly, negated instances cancel the bias outright, mul composes relative sigmas." },
    { name: "615_031 mixed-cov refusal", directory: "615_031_quantity_mixed_cov_refusal", categorySlug: "600_STDLIB/615_NUM", description: "Two different covariance groups refuse by name; one-sided tags propagate conservatively." }
  ]}
/>

## The holonomic lane — certificates, not witnesses

`std/sym` gives canonical symbolic expressions; the certificate parts on top are where the semantics get heavy. A holonomic function is one annihilated by a linear ODE with polynomial coefficients — Zeilberger's name (1990) for what Stanley called differentiably finite (1980); the friendly modern reference is Kauers & Paule's *The Concrete Tetrahedron* (2011). The annihilating ODE is a *certificate*: finite data that proves a property of an infinite object.

- `cert-seed(body)` registers a declared law for an opaque generator — `p0=[-1]|p1=[1]` is `f' − f = 0`, exp's law, given as truth.
- `derive-ode(expr, max-order, max-deg)` *discovers* a certificate by elimination over the truncated series — the "guessing" half of gfun's guess-and-prove (Salvy & Zimmermann, 1994) — witness-grade, `verified=N` honestly.
- `cert-sum`/`cert-prod` compose certificates through companion-basis elimination over ℚ(x) — the standard linear-algebra proof of the classical closure theorems, so the output is theorem-grade; [`HolonomicClosure.lean`](https://github.com/Cypoe/ISAR-proofs/blob/main/src/ISAR/HolonomicClosure.lean) checks the same statement. `cert-compose` refuses by name (`not-generally-holonomic`) because composition of D-finite functions isn't, in general.
- `cert-bridge` projects an ODE certificate into its P-recursive coefficient recurrence — the D-finite ⇔ P-recursive equivalence (Stanley 1980; Kauers & Paule §7), machine-checked as [`HolonomicBridge.lean`](https://github.com/Cypoe/ISAR-proofs/blob/main/src/ISAR/HolonomicBridge.lean); `det=1` flags exactly the hypothesis that makes the recurrence determine the sequence.
- `cert-of`/`cert-series` are the theorem upgrade: derive the ODE *structurally*, then let the recurrence *generate* the tail — every coefficient forced by the annihilating equation rather than merely witnessed.
- `decide-integral`/`decide-ode`/`decide-field` are the decision tower. A rational integrand is always elementary (Liouville): Hermite reduction in Horowitz–Ostrogradsky form builds its rational part from one square linear system over ℚ, checked exactly (`Q + (B/D⁻)' + C/D* = N/D`), and the Trager lane then builds the log part for residue cofactor polynomials up to degree 4 as an `rsum` — `1/(1+x⁴)` answers a certified residue sum, not a named gap. Non-rational integrands route through a substitution family: the dlog lane rewrites any element whose derivative the integrand carries (exp, log, sqrt, atan elements all qualify), the primitive-monomial cascade integrates polynomial-in-θ shapes like `log²x` or `atan(x)(1+atan x)`, and two global substitutions — `t = √x` and Euler's `t = x + √(x²+c)` with shift/scale pre-steps for general quadratics — rationalize whole integrands at once. Every constructed answer re-derives to the integrand, `checked-exact`; answers that can't be differentiated back (residue sums inside opaque text, non-elementary integrands like `1/(x+log x)`) refuse by named lane. `decide-ode` checks Kovacic's three necessary conditions (Kovacic, *J. Symbolic Computation* 1986) on exact pole orders and the order at infinity. It returns `proven-no-liouvillian-solution` only when all three cases fail, which is why Airy's equation is proven non-Liouvillian. When a case survives, it names the open cases instead of guessing. What the tower can't reach says `unresolved` with its search bounds named.

```koru
// x/(1+x^2)^2 -> -1/(2(1+x^2)), no log part
std/sym:lit(x*inv((1+x*x)*(1+x*x)))
| exact a |> std/sym:decide-integral(a, max-order: 3, max-deg: 4)
    | elementary m |> std/io:print.ln("{{ m:s }}")
    | holonomic-special m |> std/io:print.ln("hs:{{ m:s }}")
    | unresolved m |> std/io:print.ln("un:{{ m:s }}")
    | not-holonomic m |> std/io:print.ln("nh:{{ m:s }}")
    | non-rational |> std/io:print.ln("non-rational")
    | non-analytic |> std/io:print.ln("non-analytic")
    | bad |> std/io:print.ln("bad")
| bad |> std/io:print.ln("bad-literal")
```

That prints `decide(layer=elementary)|antiderivative=(-5e-1*inv((1e0+(x*x))))|note=hermite-constructed;checked-exact`. No sample in this post writes a `free`. Every result above carries an obligation (`<sexpr!>`, `<std/io:allocated!>`), each obligation has exactly one disposer, and the compiler inserts that disposer on every exit arm, including the arms that never touch the value.

