/>

## What refuses, on purpose

The surface's shape is defined by its edges as much as its operations. `cert-compose` refuses because composition isn't generally holonomic. `decide-ode` proves non-Liouvillian only when every Kovacic case is excluded, and otherwise names the open cases. `cert-series` on `x` says `under-determined` — its recurrence genuinely leaves a seed free. Tainted operands, mixed covariance sources, lawless generators, dim mismatches, malformed external certificates — each is a named refusal, not a silent path. Discovery is witness-grade; declaration and derivation are theorem-grade; and the line between them is drawn where the Lean proofs draw it.

Open rungs, honestly: the rational log part is built only through the residue-sum lane, and only for cofactor polynomials up to degree 4 — a quintic stays a named `rsum` refusal rather than an unreadable root tower. The substitution family covers a real but bounded slice of the elementary class: denominators in θ with no dlog factor (`1/(x+log x)`) are non-elementary by Liouville-in-the-extension and refuse honestly, mixed radicands refuse rather than partially transform. The Kovacic case algorithms, which would turn `open-cases` into constructed solutions, are not run. `g(name, arg)` application (composition and addition theorems like `exp(x+y)=exp(x)exp(y)`) needs substitution machinery — band 2. Jet relations run nonlinear laws (a Riccati `D(y)=y²`, an oscillator spelled `y'=yp, yp'=−y`, self-application `f(f)`, iterated jets feeding the derived text back through `source`), but full differential algebra — elimination, implicit relations, several variables — is outside the grammar rather than refused at runtime. And the exact-op multiplication gap versus the C bigint implementations narrows, not closes, with Karatsuba.

## Where the math comes from

- **Karatsuba & Ofman**, "Multiplication of Multidigit Numbers on Automata" (1962) — the limb multiplication.
- **Machin** (1706) — π = 16·atan(1/5) − 4·atan(1/239), the benchmark's π workload.
- **Stanley**, "Differentiably Finite Power Series" (*Europ. J. Combinatorics*, 1980; also *Enumerative Combinatorics* vol. 2, §6.4) — the D-finite theory, the closure theorems, the P-recursive equivalence.
- **Zeilberger**, "A Holonomic Systems Approach to Special Functions Identities" (*J. Comput. Appl. Math.*, 1990) — the holonomic framing the certificate lane uses.
- **Kauers & Paule**, *The Concrete Tetrahedron* (2011) — the working reference: closure proofs, series↔recurrence, guessing.
- **Salvy & Zimmermann**, "GFUN: a Maple package for the manipulation of generating and holonomic functions" (*ACM TOMS*, 1994) — the implementation lineage `derive-ode`'s discovery half descends from.
- **Kovacic**, "An Algorithm for Solving Second Order Linear Homogeneous Differential Equations" (*J. Symbolic Computation*, 1986) — the pole-structure conditions behind `decide-ode`.
- **Hermite**, "Sur l'intégration des fractions rationnelles" (1872), and **Horowitz**, "Algorithms for Partial Fraction Decomposition and Rational Function Integration" (SYMSAC 1971) — the rational-part reduction `decide-integral` runs.
- **Risch**, "The Problem of Integration in Finite Terms" (1969), and **Bronstein**, *Symbolic Integration I* (2nd ed., 2005) — the frame `decide-integral` answers honestly inside its (still smaller) reach.

The formal semantics the certificates are checked against live in Lean 4, file by file in the public [isar-proofs](https://github.com/Cypoe/ISAR-proofs) tree: [`HolonomicClosure.lean`](https://github.com/Cypoe/ISAR-proofs/blob/main/src/ISAR/HolonomicClosure.lean) (sum/product), [`HolonomicCertificate.integralShift`](https://github.com/Cypoe/ISAR-proofs/blob/main/src/ISAR/Holonomic.lean) (integration), [`HolonomicBridge.lean`](https://github.com/Cypoe/ISAR-proofs/blob/main/src/ISAR/HolonomicBridge.lean) (the P-recursive projection), [`HolonomicCompose.lean`](https://github.com/Cypoe/ISAR-proofs/blob/main/src/ISAR/HolonomicCompose.lean) (the composition obstruction), [`QuantityKernel.lean`](https://github.com/Cypoe/ISAR-proofs/blob/main/src/ISAR/QuantityKernel.lean) (the `Dim` group). Every cited proof is green — the claims in this post are checkable, which is the point.

